      "text": "Rain is one of the most fundamental and vital phenomena on Earth, serving as the primary mechanism for distributing fresh water across the planet\u2019s surface. While it may appear to be a simple event\u2014water falling from the sky\u2014the process of rain formation is a complex, multi-stage journey driven by solar energy, atmospheric physics, and thermodynamics. To truly understand how rain forms, one must examine the complete water cycle, also known as the hydrologic cycle, which describes the continuous movement of water on, above, and below the surface of the Earth. This cycle is not a linear path but a dynamic, interconnected system involving evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection.\n\nThe journey begins with **evaporation**, the process by which liquid water transforms into water vapor, a gaseous state. This phase change is primarily driven by the sun\u2019s energy. As solar radiation strikes the Earth\u2019s surface, it heats bodies of water such as oceans, lakes, rivers, and even moist soil. When water molecules absorb enough thermal energy, they gain sufficient kinetic energy to break free from the liquid surface and enter the atmosphere as invisible gas. It is estimated that approximately 90% of atmospheric water vapor originates from the evaporation of ocean water, with the remaining 10%",
